<!--
    kuna: a declared integer argument or return value narrower than its
    register is extended as the RISC-V procedure-call standard states: by the
    sign of its type up to 32 bits, then sign-extended to the register.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 calls, the call-output and entry-input extension
    (p4_calls/kuna_narrowext.rs, option narrowext).  Two passes: off is the
    compiler spec's zero extension (the bug), the default abi is the fix.

    Source (clang -target riscv64 -march=rv64gc -O2, bytes verbatim below):

        signed char rsc(int k) { return k - 100; }
        unsigned short rus(int k) { return k * 300; }
        int ri(int k) { return k * 0x10001 - 7; }
        unsigned ru(int k) { return k * 0x10001u - 7; }
        long csc(int k) { return rsc(k) * 3L; }
        long cus(int k) { return rus(k) + 1L; }
        long ci(int k) { return ri(k); }
        long cu(int k) { return (int)ru(k); }
        long pi(int x) { return x * 2L; }
        long pus(unsigned short x) { return x + 1L; }
        long pu(unsigned x) { return (int)x; }
        long psc(signed char x) { return x * 3L; }

    The callers use the callee's register as it is and the callees use their
    argument register as it is: rsc ends srai a0,a0,56 and ri ends addiw, and
    pi is slli a0,a0,1 on the incoming a0.  The spec's extension="zero" made
    ci print (unsigned long)(unsigned int)ri(k) and pi
    (unsigned long)(unsigned int)x << 1, both wrong for a negative int.
-->
